// Case (b) is not implied by "no write writes a value derived from a read",
// even when that is asked of every write in the program, not only the loop's.
//
// Every write stores a literal. Thread 2 writes y := 1 only under a branch on
// the value it read from x, so the information travels along a control
// dependency, which DP contains (the justification predicate P_j of
// app-defs.tex, def. of freeze). In an execution where the second iteration
// reads y from thread 2:
//
//   W x:=1 (iter 1) -rf-> R x (T2) -DP(ctrl)-> W y:=1 (T2) -rf-> R y (iter 2)
//
// so case (b) fails; (a) and (c) do not apply.
//
// The acquire read, release write and SC fence are there only so that
// Condition 4 holds and case (b) is the one condition at stake. Release and
// acquire alone do not order an iteration's write before the next
// iteration's read, at another location; the fence does. Without it MoRDor
// reports Condition 4 failing as well.
//
// MoRDor (63403b7) reports the loop episodic, conditions 1-4 all satisfied: its
// Condition 2 compares a read only against writes of the same loop and does
// not follow (DP u rf)+ through other threads. caseb_path.py over the
// `dependencies --output-mode json` export finds the path in 2/7 executions
// at bound 2 and 12/21 at bound 4.
rX := malloc 1;
*rX := 0;
rY := malloc 1;
*rY := 0;
{
  do
  {
    ry :acq= *rY;
    *rX :rel= 1;
    fence(sc);
  }
  while (ry = 0)
}
|||
{
  rt := *rX;
  if (rt = 1)
  {
    *rY := 1;
  }
}
